const CHEAT2 = [{"g": "C# — fundamentals, OOP aur core concepts", "items": [{"q": "What is C#?", "a": "C# is a strongly typed, object-oriented programming language mainly used with the .NET platform.", "ex": "string name = \"John\";"}, {"q": "What is .NET?", "a": ".NET is a development platform/runtime used to build Web APIs, web applications, desktop apps and services.", "ex": "ASP.NET Core Web API"}, {"q": ".NET Framework vs .NET Core", "a": ".NET Framework is the older Windows-focused platform; .NET Core introduced cross-platform support. Modern versions are called .NET.", "ex": ".NET 8 Web API"}, {"q": "CLR", "a": "CLR executes .NET code and provides Garbage Collection, exception handling, type safety and JIT compilation.", "ex": "C# → IL → CLR → Machine Code"}, {"q": "CTS", "a": "Common Type System defines how data types work across .NET languages.", "ex": "C# int → System.Int32"}, {"q": "CLS", "a": "Common Language Specification defines rules for .NET language interoperability.", "ex": "C# and VB.NET interoperability"}, {"q": "Managed Code", "a": "Code executed under CLR management.", "ex": "Normal C# code"}, {"q": "Unmanaged Code", "a": "Code executed outside CLR management, usually native code.", "ex": "C/C++ DLL"}, {"q": "JIT", "a": "Just-In-Time compiler converts IL into native machine code at runtime.", "ex": "IL → JIT → Native code"}, {"q": "OOP", "a": "Object-oriented programming organizes code around objects containing data and behavior.", "ex": "employee.CalculateSalary()"}, {"q": "Encapsulation", "a": "Data chhupao, access control karo — private field + public property.", "ex": "private decimal _salary;\npublic decimal Salary { get => _salary; set => _salary = value; }", "h": "Bank ka locker: paisa andar, chaabi control mein."}, {"q": "Inheritance", "a": "Parent ka code child mein reuse — is-a relationship.", "ex": "class Dog : Animal { }  // Dog IS-A Animal", "h": "Dog ek Animal hai. Agar 'is-a' nahi bol sakte to inheritance galat hai — composition use karo."}, {"q": "Polymorphism", "a": "Ek naam, alag behaviour. Compile-time = overloading, Runtime = overriding.", "ex": "// overload (compile-time)\nvoid Pay(int a); void Pay(decimal a, string cur);\n// override (runtime)\npublic override void Pay() { }", "h": "Poly = bahut, morph = roop. Ek method ke bahut roop."}, {"q": "Abstraction", "a": "Kya karta hai dikhao, kaise karta hai chhupao.", "ex": "IPayment p = new RazorpayPayment();\np.Pay(500);  // kaise hota hai, pata nahi", "h": "Car ka steering: ghumao, gaadi mudti hai. Andar ka mechanism chhupa hai."}, {"q": "Class", "a": "Object ka blueprint/template.", "ex": "class Employee { }"}, {"q": "Object", "a": "Class ka actual runtime instance.", "ex": "new Employee()"}, {"q": "Value Type", "a": "Value khud stack pe store hoti hai. Copy karoge to alag copy banegi.", "ex": "int a = 5; int b = a; b = 10;\n// a abhi bhi 5", "h": "int, float, bool, char, struct, enum, DateTime."}, {"q": "Reference Type", "a": "Heap pe object, stack pe uska address. Copy karoge to dono ek hi object ko point karenge.", "ex": "var a = new List<int>{1}; var b = a; b.Add(2);\n// a.Count ab 2 hai", "h": "class, interface, delegate, string, array, object."}, {"q": "Struct", "a": "User-defined value type, generally small data structures ke liye useful.", "ex": "struct Point { }"}, {"q": "Boxing", "a": "Value type ko object mein daalna — heap pe naya allocation hota hai, isliye slow.", "ex": "int i = 10;\nobject o = i;      // boxing", "h": "Box mein saman daalna = heap pe jagah leni. Loop mein boxing performance kha jati hai."}, {"q": "Unboxing", "a": "Object se wapas value type nikalna — explicit cast lagta hai.", "ex": "object o = 10;\nint i = (int)o;    // unboxing", "h": "Galat type cast karoge to InvalidCastException."}, {"q": "Nullable Type", "a": "Value type ko null accept karne deta hai.", "ex": "int? age = null;"}, {"q": "var", "a": "Compiler compile time par type infer karta hai; variable strongly typed rehta hai.", "ex": "var name = \"John\";"}, {"q": "dynamic", "a": "Type/member checking runtime par hoti hai.", "ex": "dynamic x = 10;"}, {"q": "const", "a": "Compile-time pe fix. Implicitly static. Sirf primitive/string ho sakti hai.", "ex": "public const double Pi = 3.14;", "h": "const ki value DLL mein bake ho jati hai — library update karoge to consumer ko rebuild karna padta hai."}, {"q": "readonly", "a": "Runtime constant — declaration ya constructor mein set hoti hai, phir change nahi.", "ex": "public readonly DateTime CreatedAt;\npublic Order() { CreatedAt = DateTime.Now; }", "h": "const = compile time, readonly = run time. Har object ki readonly value alag ho sakti hai."}, {"q": "static", "a": "Class-level member — saare objects mein ek hi copy shared.", "ex": "public static int Count;\nCounter.Count++;   // object banaye bina", "h": "Object ka nahi, class ka. Isliye 'new' ki zaroorat nahi."}, {"q": "sealed", "a": "Class ko aage inherit hone se rokta hai.", "ex": "public sealed class Logger { }", "h": "string sealed hai. Security ya design lock ke liye use hota hai."}, {"q": "partial", "a": "Ek class ka code multiple files mein — compile pe sab mil jata hai.", "ex": "// File1.cs\npublic partial class Order { void Save() { } }\n// File2.cs\npublic partial class Order { void Print() { } }", "h": "Designer-generated code aur apna code alag rakhne ke liye."}, {"q": "Encapsulation", "a": "Data chhupao, access control karo — private field + public property.", "ex": "private decimal _salary;\npublic decimal Salary { get => _salary; set => _salary = value; }", "h": "Bank ka locker: paisa andar, chaabi control mein."}, {"q": "Inheritance", "a": "Parent ka code child mein reuse — is-a relationship.", "ex": "class Dog : Animal { }  // Dog IS-A Animal", "h": "Dog ek Animal hai. Agar 'is-a' nahi bol sakte to inheritance galat hai — composition use karo."}, {"q": "Polymorphism", "a": "Ek naam, alag behaviour. Compile-time = overloading, Runtime = overriding.", "ex": "// overload (compile-time)\nvoid Pay(int a); void Pay(decimal a, string cur);\n// override (runtime)\npublic override void Pay() { }", "h": "Poly = bahut, morph = roop. Ek method ke bahut roop."}, {"q": "Abstraction", "a": "Kya karta hai dikhao, kaise karta hai chhupao.", "ex": "IPayment p = new RazorpayPayment();\np.Pay(500);  // kaise hota hai, pata nahi", "h": "Car ka steering: ghumao, gaadi mudti hai. Andar ka mechanism chhupa hai."}, {"q": "Method Overloading", "a": "Same naam, alag parameters. Compile time pe decide hota hai.", "ex": "void Pay(int amt);\nvoid Pay(int amt, string currency);", "h": "Sirf return type badalne se overload nahi hota — parameters badalne chahiye."}, {"q": "Method Overriding", "a": "Child class parent ka method apne tarike se likhti hai. Runtime pe decide hota hai.", "ex": "class Animal { public virtual void Speak() { } }\nclass Dog : Animal { public override void Speak() { } }", "h": "Parent mein virtual/abstract hona zaroori hai, warna override nahi kar sakte."}, {"q": "Virtual", "a": "Method ko override hone ki permission deta hai — par default implementation bhi rakhta hai.", "ex": "public virtual decimal GetDiscount() => 0;", "h": "virtual = optional override. abstract = compulsory override."}, {"q": "Abstract Method", "a": "Sirf declaration, body nahi. Child ko override karna hi padega.", "ex": "public abstract decimal GetDiscount();", "h": "Abstract method sirf abstract class mein ho sakta hai."}, {"q": "Interface", "a": "Contract/capability define karta hai.", "ex": "IRepository"}, {"q": "Abstract Class", "a": "Abstract members ke saath implementation/state bhi rakh sakti hai.", "ex": "abstract class Animal"}, {"q": "Abstract Class vs Interface", "a": "Interview answer: Abstract class tab useful hai jab related classes ke beech common state/implementation share karni ho. Interface tab useful hai jab capability/contract define karna ho.", "t": {"h": ["Abstract Class", "Interface"], "r": [["Common implementation rakh sakti hai", "Mainly contract define karta hai"], ["Fields/state rakh sakti hai", "Modern C# interfaces can contain more than only method declarations"], ["Constructor ho sakta hai", "Normal instance constructor nahi"], ["Class one base class inherit karti hai", "Multiple interfaces implement kiye ja sakte hain"], ["abstract class Animal", "interface IAnimal"]]}}, {"q": "ref", "a": "Variable ka reference bhejta hai. Caller ko pehle initialize karna padta hai.", "ex": "int x = 5;\nUpdate(ref x);     // x change ho jayega", "h": "ref = Read + Write."}, {"q": "out", "a": "Method ko value assign karni hi padegi. Caller initialize na kare to bhi chalega.", "ex": "if (int.TryParse(\"10\", out int n)) { }", "h": "out = Must Output. Ek se zyada value return karne ka tareeka."}, {"q": "in", "a": "Reference se pass hota hai par method usko change nahi kar sakta.", "ex": "void Print(in BigStruct s) { }", "h": "in = Read Only. Bade struct copy hone se bachane ke liye."}, {"q": "ref / out / in — note", "a": "Memory trick: ref = Read + Write, out = Must Output, in = Read Only."}, {"q": "==", "a": "Equality operator hai; behavior type/operator overload par depend karta hai.", "ex": "a == b"}, {"q": "Equals()", "a": "Logical/value equality define karne ke liye use/override kiya ja sakta hai.", "ex": "a.Equals(b)"}, {"q": "ReferenceEquals()", "a": "Check karta hai ki dono references same object ko point kar rahe hain ya nahi.", "ex": "ReferenceEquals(a,b)"}, {"q": "EQUALITY — note", "a": "Important: == aur Equals() always same nahi hote."}, {"q": "Collections", "a": "Concept Answer Example ------------ ----------------------------------- ------------------------ Array Fixed-size collection. int[] a = {1,2,3}; List Dynamically resizable collection. List<int> list Dictionary Key-value lookup collection. Dictionary<int,string> HashSet Unique values maintain karta hai. HashSet<int> Stack LIFO — Last In First Out. Push() / Pop() Queue FIFO — First In First Out. Enqueue() / Dequeue()"}, {"q": "IEnumerable vs IQueryable vs List", "a": "Interview answer: IEnumerable generally works with in-memory data, IQueryable allows a provider like EF Core to translate the query to the database, and List represents an actual materialized collection. Example: IQueryable<User> query = db.Users; query = query.Where(x => x.IsActive); var users = query.ToList(); Important: ToList() generally causes query execution/materialization.", "t": {"h": ["", "IEnumerable", "IQueryable", "List"], "r": [["Purpose", "Enumeration", "Query building", "Actual collection"], ["Execution", "Usually in-memory", "Provider/data-source side", "Already materialized"], ["DB translation", "No", "Yes, commonly", "No"], ["Deferred execution", "Often", "Yes", "No"], ["Example", "IEnumerable<User>", "IQueryable<User>", "List<User>"]]}}, {"q": "Where()", "a": "Filter", "ex": "users.Where(x => x.Age > 18)"}, {"q": "Select()", "a": "Projection/transformation", "ex": "users.Select(x => x.Name)"}, {"q": "SelectMany()", "a": "Flatten nested collections", "ex": "orders.SelectMany(x => x.Items)"}, {"q": "First()", "a": "First item; throws if none", "ex": "users.First()"}, {"q": "FirstOrDefault()", "a": "First/default if none", "ex": "users.FirstOrDefault()"}, {"q": "Single()", "a": "Exactly one expected", "ex": "users.Single(x => x.Id == 1)"}, {"q": "SingleOrDefault()", "a": "Zero/one allowed", "ex": "users.SingleOrDefault()"}, {"q": "Any()", "a": "Existence check", "ex": "users.Any()"}, {"q": "Count()", "a": "Number of records", "ex": "users.Count()"}, {"q": "OrderBy()", "a": "Ascending sort", "ex": ".OrderBy(x => x.Name)"}, {"q": "OrderByDescending()", "a": "Descending sort", "ex": ".OrderByDescending(x => x.Salary)"}, {"q": "GroupBy()", "a": "Groups records", "ex": ".GroupBy(x => x.Department)"}, {"q": "ToList()", "a": "Materializes result", "ex": ".ToList()"}, {"q": "LINQ — note", "a": "Deferred execution: LINQ query immediately execute nahi hoti; execution generally enumeration/materialization jaise ToList(), First() etc. par hota hai."}, {"q": "Garbage Collection", "a": "Heap ki unreachable memory automatically free karti hai. Stack ko GC nahi chhuti.", "ex": "// koi reference nahi bacha\nvar o = new Order(); o = null;", "h": "Gen 0 (naye, sabse zyada saaf hote hain) → Gen 1 → Gen 2 (lambe chalne wale)."}, {"q": "Gen 0", "a": "Short-lived objects.", "ex": "Temporary objects"}, {"q": "Gen 1", "a": "Objects surviving Gen 0.", "ex": "Medium-lived objects"}, {"q": "Gen 2", "a": "Long-lived objects.", "ex": "Long-lived application objects"}, {"q": "IDisposable", "a": "Explicit resource cleanup contract.", "ex": "DB connection/file"}, {"q": "using", "a": "Automatically Dispose call karta hai.", "ex": "using var conn = ..."}, {"q": "GARBAGE COLLECTION — note", "a": "Does GC immediately remove an unused object? No. Object unreachable hone ke baad GC eventually collect karta hai; timing runtime decide karta hai."}, {"q": "async", "a": "Method ko awaitable banata hai. Thread block nahi hota, wapas pool mein chala jata hai.", "ex": "public async Task<Order> GetAsync(int id)\n{ return await _repo.FindAsync(id); }", "h": "async naya thread NAHI banata. Yeh sabse common galat jawab hai."}, {"q": "await", "a": "Task complete hone tak method suspend karta hai, par thread free kar deta hai.", "ex": "var data = await http.GetStringAsync(url);", "h": "await ke baad ka code continuation ban jata hai — compiler state machine banata hai."}, {"q": "Task", "a": "Ek asynchronous operation ko represent karta hai. Thread ke barabar nahi hota.", "ex": "Task<int> t = Task.Run(() => Heavy());", "h": "Task = kaam. Thread = worker. Ek thread kai task chala sakta hai."}, {"q": "Task", "a": "Ek asynchronous operation ko represent karta hai. Thread ke barabar nahi hota.", "ex": "Task<int> t = Task.Run(() => Heavy());", "h": "Task = kaam. Thread = worker. Ek thread kai task chala sakta hai."}, {"q": "Async I/O", "a": "Waiting ke time thread block nahi hota.", "ex": "DB/API call"}, {"q": "Parallelism", "a": "CPU work concurrently execute karna.", "ex": "Parallel.ForEach()"}, {"q": "ASYNC / AWAIT — note", "a": "Important: async automatically new thread create nahi karta. I/O operation wait karte waqt thread efficiently release ho sakta hai."}, {"q": "Transient", "a": "Har baar inject karne pe bilkul naya instance.", "ex": "services.AddTransient<IEmailSender, EmailSender>();", "h": "Lightweight, stateless services ke liye."}, {"q": "Scoped", "a": "Har HTTP request pe ek naya instance. DbContext ka default yahi hai.", "ex": "services.AddScoped<IOrderRepository, OrderRepository>();", "h": "Ek request ke andar jitni baar inject karo, wahi instance milega."}, {"q": "Singleton", "a": "Poori application mein ek hi instance, app band hone tak zinda.", "ex": "services.AddSingleton<ICacheService, CacheService>();", "h": "Singleton mein Scoped inject mat karo — DbContext phansega (captive dependency)."}, {"q": "DEPENDENCY INJECTION — note", "a": "DI means class apni dependency khud create nahi karti; dependency container se inject hoti hai. Benefits: loose coupling, testability, maintainability. Example: builder.Services.AddScoped<IUserService, UserService>();"}, {"q": "S — Srp", "a": "Class ka single responsibility hona chahiye.", "ex": "EmailService only email"}, {"q": "O — Ocp", "a": "Stable existing code ko modify kiye bina extension possible ho.", "ex": "Interface + new implementations"}, {"q": "L — Lsp", "a": "Child parent ki jagah safely use ho sake.", "ex": "Dog : Animal"}, {"q": "I — Isp", "a": "Large interface ki jagah focused interfaces.", "ex": "IReadable, IWritable"}, {"q": "D — Dip", "a": "High-level code abstractions par depend kare.", "ex": "IRepository"}, {"q": "Dispose", "a": "Unmanaged resources (file, DB connection, socket) turant chhodne ke liye — developer call karta hai.", "ex": "using var conn = new NpgsqlConnection(cs);\n// block khatam = Dispose auto", "h": "Dispose deterministic hai (kab chalega pata hai), Finalize nahi."}, {"q": "Finalize", "a": "GC call karti hai, kab chalega pata nahi. Isliye Dispose preferred hai.", "ex": "~Order() { }   // finalizer", "h": "Finalizer object ko ek extra GC cycle tak zinda rakhta hai — performance cost."}, {"q": "Deadlock", "a": ".Result ya .Wait() se async code sync context mein block ho jata hai.", "ex": "// GALAT\nvar x = GetAsync().Result;\n// SAHI\nvar x = await GetAsync();", "h": "Async all the way — beech mein sync mat karo."}]}, {"g": "ASP.NET Core — pipeline, API, auth", "items": [{"q": "ASP.NET Core", "a": "Cross-platform framework for APIs/web applications.", "ex": ".NET Web API"}, {"q": "Program.cs", "a": "Application startup/configuration.", "ex": "builder.Services.AddControllers()"}, {"q": "appsettings.json", "a": "Configuration settings.", "ex": "Connection strings"}, {"q": "Middleware", "a": "Har request ek pipeline se guzarti hai — order matter karta hai.", "ex": "app.UseRouting();\napp.UseAuthentication();\napp.UseAuthorization();\napp.MapControllers();", "h": "Authentication hamesha Authorization se PEHLE. Ulta kiya to sab 401."}, {"q": "Routing", "a": "URL → endpoint mapping.", "ex": "/api/users/10"}, {"q": "Attribute Routing", "a": "Attribute se route define.", "ex": "[HttpGet(\"{id}\")]"}, {"q": "Model Binding", "a": "Request data → C# model.", "ex": "[FromBody] UserDto"}, {"q": "Model Validation", "a": "Incoming model validation.", "ex": "[Required]"}, {"q": "ControllerBase", "a": "API controller base class.", "ex": "UserController : ControllerBase"}, {"q": "Controller", "a": "MVC controller with view support.", "ex": "HomeController : Controller"}, {"q": "Middleware Pipeline", "a": "Typical flow: HTTP Request → Exception Middleware → HTTPS → Authentication → Authorization → Routing → Controller → Response Interview answer: Middleware request pipeline ke components hain. Request registered middleware ke through sequentially pass hoti hai, aur middleware request/response ko inspect ya modify kar sakta hai. Why order matters: Authentication should happen before authorization, and exception handling should wrap downstream components."}, {"q": "Global Exception Handling", "a": "Interview answer: Global exception middleware use karke unhandled exceptions centrally catch karta hoon, logs maintain karta hoon aur client ko consistent error response deta hoon. Typical structure: try { await _next(context); } catch (Exception ex) { _logger.LogError(ex, \"Unhandled exception\"); }"}, {"q": "Web API", "a": "Method Meaning Example -------- --------------------- ---------------------- GET Read data GET /api/users POST Create POST /api/users PUT Full update/replace PUT /api/users/10 PATCH Partial update PATCH /api/users/10 DELETE Delete DELETE /api/users/10 PUT generally complete resource replacement/update ke liye aur PATCH partial modification ke liye use hota hai."}, {"q": "HTTP Status Codes", "a": "Code Meaning Example ------ ----------------- ----------------------- 200 Success GET successful 201 Created New user created 204 Success/no body Delete successful 400 Bad Request Invalid input 401 Unauthenticated Invalid/missing JWT 403 Forbidden User lacks permission 404 Not Found User doesn’t exist 409 Conflict Duplicate email 500 Server error Unhandled exception 401 = authentication problem. 403 = authenticated but insufficient permission."}, {"q": "Jwt Authentication", "a": "Flow: Angular Login → POST /login → .NET validates credentials → JWT generated → Angular receives token → HTTP Interceptor → Authorization: Bearer TOKEN → .NET token validation → [Authorize] → API JWT is a signed token containing claims. Client sends it as a Bearer token, and ASP.NET Core validates the token before allowing access to protected endpoints. Authentication = Who are you? Authorization = What are you allowed to do?", "ex": "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...", "h": "JWT encrypted nahi hota, sirf signed hai. Payload koi bhi base64-decode kar sakta hai — password mat daalo."}, {"q": "Action Filters", "a": "Filter Purpose Example ---------------------- --------------------------- ----------------- Authorization Filter Authorization check Role validation Resource Filter Around resource execution Caching Action Filter Before/after action Logging Exception Filter MVC action exceptions Error logging Middleware works at the HTTP pipeline level; filters are more closely associated with MVC/controller/action execution."}, {"q": "DTO", "a": "Sirf zaroori data bahar bhejta hai. Entity kabhi seedha API se return mat karo.", "ex": "public record OrderDto(int Id, decimal Amount);", "h": "Entity return karoge to password, internal flags aur navigation properties leak ho jayengi."}, {"q": "IEnumerable", "a": "In-memory collection pe kaam karta hai. Filter memory mein hota hai, DB pe nahi.", "ex": "IEnumerable<Order> o = db.Orders.ToList();\no.Where(x => x.Amount > 100);  // saara data pehle aa gaya", "h": "Yeh interview ka favourite trap hai — galat use karoge to poora table memory mein aa jayega."}, {"q": "IQueryable", "a": "Expression tree banata hai aur DB pe SQL bhejta hai. Filter DB pe chalta hai.", "ex": "IQueryable<Order> o = db.Orders;\no.Where(x => x.Amount > 100);  // WHERE SQL mein gaya", "h": "IQueryable = DB pe kaam, IEnumerable = memory mein kaam."}]}, {"g": "Entity Framework Core", "items": [{"q": "EF Core", "a": "ORM for .NET", "ex": "DbContext"}, {"q": "DbContext", "a": "DB interaction/change tracking", "ex": "AppDbContext"}, {"q": "DbSet", "a": "Entity/table access", "ex": "DbSet<User>"}, {"q": "Migration", "a": "Schema change management", "ex": "Add-Migration Initial"}, {"q": "Code First", "a": "Model → database", "ex": "C# model"}, {"q": "Database First", "a": "Existing DB → models", "ex": "Scaffold"}, {"q": "Change Tracking", "a": "Detects entity changes", "ex": "user.Name = \"X\""}, {"q": "AsNoTracking", "a": "Change tracking off — read-only queries kaafi fast ho jati hain.", "ex": "db.Orders.AsNoTracking().ToList();", "h": "Reports aur GET APIs mein hamesha lagao. Update karna ho to mat lagao."}, {"q": "Include", "a": "Eager loading", "ex": ".Include(x => x.Department)"}, {"q": "Lazy Loading", "a": "Navigation property tab load hoti hai jab use karo — chupke se extra query chalti hai.", "ex": "var o = db.Orders.First();\nvar name = o.Customer.Name;  // yahan naya SQL", "h": "Lazy loading loop mein chala to N+1 problem ban jati hai."}, {"q": "Explicit Loading", "a": "Manually load relationship", "ex": ".Load()"}, {"q": "SaveChanges", "a": "Persist changes", "ex": "SaveChangesAsync()"}, {"q": "Lazy Vs Eager Vs Explicit", "a": "Type Meaning Example ---------- ----------------------------- ----------------------------- Lazy Load when property accessed user.Department Eager Load with main query .Include(x => x.Department) Explicit Developer manually loads .Reference(...).Load() For predictable API/database performance, choose loading strategy based on actual requirements. Blind lazy loading can create N+1 queries.", "ex": "db.Orders.Include(o => o.Customer).ToList();", "h": "N+1 ka standard ilaaj."}, {"q": "N+1 Problem", "a": "Example: 1 query → Get 100 users 100 queries → Get each user’s department Total = 101 database queries. Better: db.Users.Include(x => x.Department).ToListAsync(); Interview answer: N+1 occurs when one query loads parent records and additional queries execute for each parent. It can be solved through appropriate joins, eager loading or projection.", "ex": "// 100 orders + 100 customer queries = 101\nforeach (var o in orders) Console.Write(o.Customer.Name);", "h": "Fix: .Include() ya projection (.Select) se ek hi query."}, {"q": "AsNoTracking()", "a": "Example: var users = await db.Users.AsNoTracking().ToListAsync(); Answer: For read-only queries, AsNoTracking disables EF Core change tracking, reducing tracking overhead."}]}, {"g": "Database aur SQL", "items": [{"q": "Primary Key", "a": "Unique row identifier", "ex": "id PRIMARY KEY"}, {"q": "Foreign Key", "a": "Table relationship", "ex": "dept_id REFERENCES department(id)"}, {"q": "Unique", "a": "Prevent duplicates", "ex": "email UNIQUE"}, {"q": "Not Null", "a": "Value required", "ex": "name NOT NULL"}, {"q": "Default", "a": "Default value", "ex": "is_active DEFAULT true"}, {"q": "Check", "a": "Validates condition", "ex": "salary > 0"}, {"q": "View", "a": "Virtual table based on query", "ex": "CREATE VIEW"}, {"q": "Materialized View", "a": "Stored query result", "ex": "REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW"}, {"q": "Inner Join", "a": "Dono tables mein jo match kare sirf wahi.", "ex": "SELECT o.id, c.name FROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id;", "h": "Bina match wali rows gayab ho jati hain — yahi LEFT JOIN se farak hai."}, {"q": "Left Join", "a": "Left table ki saari rows + right ka match. Match na ho to NULL.", "ex": "SELECT c.name, o.id FROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;", "h": "WHERE o.id IS NULL laga do to 'jinhone kabhi order nahi kiya' mil jayenge."}, {"q": "Right Join", "a": "All right records + matching left records", "ex": "A RIGHT JOIN B"}, {"q": "Full Join", "a": "All matching/non-matching records from both sides", "ex": "A FULL JOIN B"}, {"q": "Where vs Having", "a": "Clause Purpose Example -------- ----------------------------------- ---------------------- WHERE Filters rows before grouping WHERE salary > 50000 HAVING Filters grouped/aggregate results HAVING COUNT(*) > 5"}, {"q": "Indexes", "a": "An index helps PostgreSQL locate rows faster, especially for frequently filtered, joined or sorted columns. Example: CREATE INDEX idx_vehicle_date ON vehicle_dtls(vehicle_id, created_date); Advantages: faster reads/searches. Disadvantages: extra storage and INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE overhead. Should every column have an index? No. Use query patterns and execution plans to decide.", "ex": "CREATE INDEX idx_orders_date ON orders(order_date);", "h": "Trade-off: SELECT fast, INSERT/UPDATE slow, extra disk. Har column pe index lagana galat hai."}, {"q": "Explain Analyze", "a": "Example: EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM vehicle_dtls WHERE vehicle_id = 100; It shows actual execution statistics including execution time, actual rows, scan type and join strategy. Interview answer: I use EXPLAIN ANALYZE to understand how PostgreSQL actually executes a query and identify scans, joins, row estimates and execution time."}, {"q": "Transactions", "a": "Example bank transfer: Debit Account A → Credit Account B Both should succeed or both fail. BEGIN; UPDATE account SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1; UPDATE account SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2; COMMIT; If something fails: ROLLBACK;", "ex": "BEGIN;\nINSERT ...; INSERT ...;\nCOMMIT;  -- ya ROLLBACK", "h": "Stored proc mein 10 dependent insert ho to poora block transaction mein daalo."}, {"q": "Atomicity", "a": "All or nothing", "ex": "Bank transfer"}, {"q": "Consistency", "a": "Valid state maintain", "ex": "Balance rules"}, {"q": "Isolation", "a": "Concurrent transactions don’t incorrectly interfere", "ex": "Concurrent updates"}, {"q": "Durability", "a": "Committed data survives failures", "ex": "Committed payment"}, {"q": "Delete", "a": "Selected rows delete; WHERE can be used", "ex": "DELETE FROM users WHERE id=10"}, {"q": "Truncate", "a": "All rows quickly remove", "ex": "TRUNCATE users"}, {"q": "Drop", "a": "Table/object itself remove", "ex": "DROP TABLE users"}, {"q": "PostgreSQL MVCC / VACUUM", "a": "MVCC means PostgreSQL maintains row versions to support concurrent transactions. DELETE does not necessarily immediately shrink the physical table file. VACUUM identifies dead tuples and makes their space reusable internally. VACUUM FULL rewrites the table and can reclaim OS-level disk space, but is heavier and requires planning. Flow: DELETE → dead tuples → VACUUM → reusable space.", "ex": "-- dead tuples rehte hain\nVACUUM orders;", "h": "Isliye DELETE ke baad disk kam nahi hoti. VACUUM FULL OS ko jagah wapas deta hai par table lock karta hai."}, {"q": "2nd Highest Salary", "a": "SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employee WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employee);"}, {"q": "Duplicate Records", "a": "SELECT email, COUNT(*) FROM employee GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;"}, {"q": "Department Employee Count", "a": "SELECT department_id, COUNT(*) FROM employee GROUP BY department_id;"}, {"q": "Department Highest Salary", "a": "SELECT department_id, MAX(salary) FROM employee GROUP BY department_id;"}, {"q": "Last 5 Days", "a": "SELECT * FROM vehicle_dtls WHERE created_date >= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '5 days';"}, {"q": "Find NULL", "a": "SELECT * FROM employee WHERE manager_id IS NULL;"}, {"q": "Duplicate rows with ROW_NUMBER", "a": "Use ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) and filter rn > 1."}, {"q": "Acid", "a": "Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.", "ex": "BEGIN; UPDATE a...; UPDATE b...; COMMIT;", "h": "Paisa transfer: A se kata aur B mein nahi pahuncha — Atomicity toot gayi."}]}, {"g": "Angular aur RxJS", "items": [{"q": "Angular Fundamentals", "a": "Concept Meaning Example ---------------- ------------------------------- -------------------------------------- Angular TypeScript frontend framework ng new app Component UI + logic reusable block UserComponent Module Logical grouping UserModule Service Shared/business/API logic UserService Directive DOM behavior/structure *ngIf Pipe Display transformation uppercase Lifecycle Hook Component lifecycle stage ngOnInit() Constructor DI/setup constructor(private api: ApiService) ngOnInit Initialization logic this.loadUsers()"}, {"q": "Angular Directives", "a": "Type Meaning Example ---------------------- ----------------------------- --------------- Structural Directive Changes DOM structure *ngIf, *ngFor Attribute Directive Changes appearance/behavior [ngClass]"}, {"q": "Angular Data Binding", "a": "Type Meaning Example ------------------ ----------------- ---------------------- Interpolation Display value {{name}} Property Binding Component → DOM [disabled]=\"loading\" Event Binding DOM → Component (click)=\"save()\" Two-way Binding Both directions [(ngModel)]=\"name\" Memory: Interpolation = Show Property = Set Event = Listen Two-way = Both"}, {"q": "Parent → Child", "a": "@Input()", "ex": "@Input() user: User"}, {"q": "Child → Parent", "a": "@Output() + EventEmitter", "ex": "@Output() saved = new EventEmitter()"}, {"q": "Sibling/shared", "a": "Shared service or state", "ex": "UserStateService"}, {"q": "ANGULAR RxJS", "a": "Concept Meaning Example ---------------------- ------------------------------------ ----------------------- Observable Async data stream http.get() Subject Multicast observable + observer Subject<string> BehaviorSubject Stores latest value BehaviorSubject<User> map Transform values map(x => x.name) filter Filter values filter(x => x.active) switchMap Previous stream replaced by latest Search mergeMap Inner streams can run concurrently Independent requests concatMap Inner streams run sequentially Ordered requests debounceTime Wait for inactivity Search box distinctUntilChanged Ignore same consecutive values Search catchError Handle errors HTTP failure"}, {"q": "switchMap", "a": "Purani inner subscription cancel karke nayi shuru karta hai.", "ex": "search$.pipe(debounceTime(300), switchMap(q => api.search(q)))", "h": "Search box ka default jawab — purana result aake naya overwrite nahi karega."}, {"q": "mergeMap", "a": "Saari inner subscriptions parallel chalne deta hai, order ki guarantee nahi.", "ex": "ids$.pipe(mergeMap(id => api.get(id)))", "h": "Independent parallel calls ke liye."}, {"q": "concatMap", "a": "Ek ke baad ek, order maintain karke.", "ex": "saves$.pipe(concatMap(x => api.save(x)))", "h": "Jab sequence important ho — jaise ordered save."}, {"q": "switchMap VS mergeMap VS concatMap — note", "a": "Search example: valueChanges.pipe(debounceTime(300), distinctUntilChanged(), switchMap(value => this.search(value)))"}, {"q": "Angular HTTP", "a": "Concept Meaning Example ------------- ------------------------------------ ---------------------------- HttpClient Backend communication http.get() GET Read http.get('/users') POST Create http.post('/users', data) PUT Update http.put('/users/1', data) DELETE Remove http.delete('/users/1') Interceptor Global request/response processing JWT catchError Error handling catchError()"}, {"q": "Http Interceptor", "a": "Typical flow: Angular API call → HTTP Interceptor → Attach JWT → .NET API. Uses: - JWT attachment - Logging - Loading indicator - Global error handling - 401 handling", "ex": "intercept(req, next) {\n  return next.handle(req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${t}` } }));\n}", "h": "JWT attach karne ki standard jagah."}, {"q": "Angular Forms", "a": "Concept Meaning Example ----------------- ------------------------------ ---------------------- Template-driven Form mostly HTML-driven [(ngModel)] Reactive Form structure in TypeScript FormGroup FormControl Single form field new FormControl() FormGroup Collection of controls new FormGroup({...}) Validator Validation rule Validators.required Simple forms can use template-driven forms; complex/dynamic validation is often better suited to reactive forms."}, {"q": "Change Detection", "a": "Angular detects component state changes and updates the required DOM. OnPush reduces unnecessary checks by limiting change detection to relevant triggers such as input reference changes, events and explicit marking. Example: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush"}, {"q": "ngIf VS hidden", "t": {"h": ["*ngIf", "[hidden]"], "r": [["Element can be removed from DOM", "Element remains in DOM"], ["Component can be destroyed/recreated", "Component generally remains alive"], ["Conditional rendering", "Visual hiding"]]}}, {"q": "Angular Performance", "a": "Technique Purpose --------------------- ------------------------------------- Lazy Loading Reduce initial bundle OnPush Reduce unnecessary change detection trackBy Reduce DOM recreation Debounce Reduce API calls Pagination Reduce large data rendering Unsubscribe/cleanup Avoid long-lived subscription leaks Caching Reduce repeated requests"}, {"q": "Routing / Guards", "a": "Concept Meaning Example ----------------- ------------------------------- ------------------------------ Route Guard Route access control AuthGuard CanActivate Check before route activation canActivate CanDeactivate Prevent leaving route Unsaved form Route Parameter Dynamic URL value /users/:id Lazy Route Load route on demand loadChildren / loadComponent"}, {"q": "NgRx", "a": "Concept Meaning Example ---------- --------------------------- ---------------------- Store Central application state userState Action Event/intent loadUsers() Reducer Creates next state on(loadSuccess, ...) Effect Side effects/API calls loadUsers$ Selector Reads/derives state selectUsers NgRx is useful for complex/shared application state; simple apps may not need it."}, {"q": "ngOnInit", "a": "Component ke @Input set hone ke baad ek baar chalta hai. API call yahan.", "ex": "ngOnInit() { this.svc.getOrders().subscribe(d => this.orders = d); }", "h": "Constructor mein API call mat karo — wahan Input abhi set nahi hue hote."}, {"q": "ngOnDestroy", "a": "Component hatne se pehle cleanup — warna memory leak.", "ex": "private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();\nngOnDestroy() { this.destroy$.next(); this.destroy$.complete(); }", "h": "takeUntil(this.destroy$) pattern sabse saaf hai."}, {"q": "Observable", "a": "Stream jo time ke saath kai values de sakta hai. Lazy aur cancellable.", "ex": "this.http.get(url).subscribe(d => this.data = d);", "h": "Subscribe nahi karoge to HTTP call hoti hi nahi — Observable lazy hai."}, {"q": "Promise", "a": "Ek hi value, turant chal padta hai, cancel nahi hota.", "ex": "fetch(url).then(r => r.json());", "h": "Observable = stream + cancellable. Promise = single + non-cancellable."}, {"q": "OnPush", "a": "Component sirf tab check hota hai jab @Input reference badle ya event aaye.", "ex": "@Component({ changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush })", "h": "Object mutate karoge to view update nahi hoga — naya object banao."}]}, {"g": "Architecture, Docker, CI/CD", "items": [{"q": "Monolith vs Microservices", "a": "Microservices should be introduced when their benefits justify the added operational complexity.", "t": {"h": ["Monolith", "Microservices"], "r": [["One deployable application", "Multiple independently deployable services"], ["Simpler initially", "More operational complexity"], ["Easier local debugging", "Distributed debugging"], ["Scaling often application-level", "Services can scale independently"], ["Usually simpler deployment", "More networking/monitoring concerns"]]}}, {"q": "Microservices", "a": "Typical: API Gateway → User Service → Order Service → Payment Service Each service can have its own data store. Important principles: - Independent deployment - Service ownership - API/message communication - Fault isolation - Independent scaling - Monitoring/logging"}, {"q": "Synchronous", "a": "Caller waits for response", "ex": "REST API"}, {"q": "Asynchronous", "a": "Caller doesn’t need immediate completion", "ex": "Kafka event"}, {"q": "SYNCHRONOUS VS ASYNCHRONOUS — note", "a": "Example: Order Created → Kafka Event → Notification Service."}, {"q": "API Gateway", "a": "API Gateway client aur backend microservices ke beech single entry point provide karta hai. Common responsibilities: - Routing - Authentication - Rate limiting - Request aggregation - Logging - Sometimes load balancing"}, {"q": "CQRS", "a": "CQRS = Command Query Responsibility Segregation. Command = change/write data. Query = read data. Examples: CreateOrderCommand GetOrderQuery Use when read/write responsibilities or workloads are sufficiently different to justify the additional architecture."}, {"q": "Clean Architecture", "a": "Typical: Presentation → Application → Domain ← Infrastructure Layer Responsibility Example ---------------- --------------------- -------------------- Presentation API/UI UserController Application Use cases CreateUserHandler Domain Business rules User, Order Infrastructure DB/external systems PostgresRepository Goal: business logic ko database/framework/UI se independent rakhna."}, {"q": "Caching", "a": "Type Meaning Example -------------------- --------------------------------- -------------------- IMemoryCache One application instance memory _cache.Set() Distributed Cache Shared between instances Redis Cache Aside Cache first, DB on miss Redis → PostgreSQL Cache Invalidation Remove/update stale cache _cache.Remove() Cache Aside flow: Request → Cache? → If yes return → If no DB → Store in cache → Return."}, {"q": "gRPC", "a": "gRPC is a high-performance RPC framework using HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers. REST gRPC ----------------------------- ----------------------------------------- Commonly JSON Protobuf HTTP APIs HTTP/2 Browser/public API friendly Strong service-to-service fit Human-readable payload Compact binary payload Simple APIs High-performance internal communication"}, {"q": "Docker", "a": "Concept Answer Example -------------- ---------------------------------------- ---------------------- Docker Application packaging/runtime platform docker run api Image Immutable application template my-api:1.0 Container Running image instance docker ps Dockerfile Image build instructions FROM ... Compose Multiple services management docker compose up -d Network Container communication api → postgres Volume Persistent storage postgres_data Port Mapping Host → container 8080:80 Logs Container logs docker logs api Stats Resource usage docker stats", "ex": "docker build -t api .\ndocker run -p 8080:80 api", "h": "'Mere machine pe to chal raha tha' ka ilaaj."}, {"q": "Exit Code 137", "a": "Exit 137 means the process/container was killed with SIGKILL. One common cause is memory/OOM pressure. Check: docker stats docker inspect <container> docker logs <container>"}, {"q": "Nginx", "a": "Nginx can act as: - Web server - Reverse proxy - SSL terminator - Load balancer Typical: Browser → Nginx :443 → .NET API :5000"}, {"q": "CI/CD / Jenkins", "a": "Typical pipeline: Developer → Git → Jenkins → Build → Test → Docker Image → Deploy → Health Check Jenkins automates CI/CD tasks such as build, test and deployment."}]}, {"g": "Production scenarios — kya poocha jata hai", "items": [{"q": "Production: API Slow", "a": "Troubleshooting order: 1. Check API logs 2. Check response time 3. Check database query 4. Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE 5. Check indexes 6. Check CPU/RAM 7. Check DB connections 8. Check network/external APIs 9. Check recent deployment 10. Verify after fix Avoid simply restarting the server without finding the root cause."}, {"q": "Production: Database Full", "a": "Investigation: df -h → database size → largest tables → indexes → WAL → logs → temporary files → bloat Then choose appropriate remediation such as cleanup, archival, partitioning, vacuuming or storage expansion."}, {"q": "Delete But Disk Not Reducing", "a": "Strong answer: PostgreSQL uses MVCC, so DELETE creates dead tuples rather than immediately shrinking the physical table. VACUUM makes space reusable internally; VACUUM FULL/table rewrite can reclaim OS-level space but is heavier and requires planning."}, {"q": "Some Users Login, Some Don’T", "a": "If requests go through a load balancer, compare backend instances individually: - application logs - authentication logs - configuration - health checks - recent deployments If one instance is unhealthy, isolate/fix it and verify the issue."}, {"q": "API 500", "a": "Troubleshooting: 500 → Application logs → Stack trace → Recent changes → DB/external service → Reproduce → Root cause → Fix → Verify"}, {"q": "Angular Slow", "a": "Check: 1. Network 2. API response time 3. Payload size 4. Number of API calls 5. Change detection 6. Large ngFor 7. RxJS subscriptions 8. Expensive template functions 9. Bundle size 10. Lazy loading Possible fixes: OnPush, trackBy, pagination, lazy loading, debounce, caching, API optimization."}, {"q": "Search API Every Keystroke", "a": "Bad: A → API An → API Ang → API Angu → API Angular → API Better: valueChanges.pipe(debounceTime(300), distinctUntilChanged(), switchMap(value => this.search(value))) Answer: debounceTime reduces rapid calls, distinctUntilChanged removes duplicates, switchMap prioritizes the latest search."}, {"q": "Large API Response", "a": "Use: - Pagination - Filtering - Sorting - DTO projection - Compression where appropriate - Caching Example: GET /vehicles?page=1&pageSize=20 Response can contain: data, totalCount, page, pageSize."}]}, {"g": "Project, performance aur security answers", "items": [{"q": "Project Architecture", "a": "Typical: Angular → HttpClient → Interceptor → Nginx / Load Balancer → .NET Web API → Controller → Service → Repository/DB Layer → PostgreSQL Important: Explain only layers and technologies you actually used in your project."}, {"q": "Explain One API", "a": "Use this sequence: Request → Controller → Validation → Service/Business Logic → Database Function/Query → Data Mapping → DTO → HTTP Response Example: GET /api/vehicles"}, {"q": "Pagination", "a": "Frontend sends: page = 2 pageSize = 10 Backend: skip = (page - 1) × pageSize For page 2: skip = 10 PostgreSQL: SELECT * FROM vehicle_dtls ORDER BY id LIMIT 10 OFFSET 10; Server-side pagination prevents loading the entire dataset into application memory."}, {"q": "Logging", "a": "Use structured logs for important events, exceptions, DB errors and business operations. Example: _logger.LogError(ex, \"Failed to fetch vehicle {VehicleId}\", vehicleId); Centralized logging can be analyzed using Elasticsearch/Kibana where available."}, {"q": "Difficult Production Issue", "a": "Use STAR: Situation → Task → Action → Result Example: Production API was slow. Logs showed most time spent in a database query. I analyzed the execution plan, identified the problematic query/index strategy, optimized it and verified the response time after deployment. Use real measurable impact if you genuinely have it."}, {"q": "Security", "a": "API security topics: - HTTPS - JWT/OAuth2 - Authentication - Authorization - Input validation - CORS - Rate limiting where required - Secure secrets/configuration - Parameterized queries - Logging/auditing SQL injection is prevented using parameterized queries/ORM query APIs rather than string concatenation."}, {"q": "API Performance", "a": "Possible improvements: 1. Database query optimization 2. Proper indexes 3. Async I/O 4. Pagination 5. DTO projection 6. Caching 7. Reduce unnecessary external calls 8. Proper resource management 9. Compression where appropriate 10. Monitoring/profiling"}, {"q": "Database Performance", "a": "For a slow PostgreSQL query: 1. Reproduce 2. EXPLAIN ANALYZE 3. Check scans 4. Check joins 5. Check filters 6. Check indexes 7. Check row estimates 8. Reduce unnecessary columns 9. Check data volume/statistics 10. Re-test"}]}, {"g": "Trick questions — yahan log fisalte hain", "items": [{"q": "Does async create a new thread?", "a": "No, not automatically."}, {"q": "Is IQueryable always faster than IEnumerable?", "a": "No. It depends on provider, query and data."}, {"q": "Should every DB column have an index?", "a": "No. Indexes have storage and write overhead."}, {"q": "Does DELETE always release disk space?", "a": "No. PostgreSQL MVCC means physical space may remain allocated."}, {"q": "Is Singleton always best for performance?", "a": "No. Lifetime must match resource/thread-safety requirements."}, {"q": "Should microservices be used everywhere?", "a": "No. Their complexity should be justified by requirements."}, {"q": "Is JWT encrypted?", "a": "A normal JWT is signed, not inherently encrypted."}, {"q": "Is authentication the same as authorization?", "a": "No. Authentication identifies; authorization controls access."}, {"q": "Does ToList() execute IQueryable?", "a": "Generally yes; it materializes the query."}, {"q": "Is AsNoTracking always faster?", "a": "It can reduce tracking overhead for read-only queries, but context matters."}, {"q": "Is Angular *ngIf same as [hidden]?", "a": "No. *ngIf changes DOM presence; [hidden] hides an existing element."}, {"q": "Does every Observable require manual unsubscribe?", "a": "No. Many HTTP Observables complete automatically; long-lived subscriptions need cleanup."}, {"q": "Is throw same as throw ex?", "a": "No. throw preserves the original stack trace better."}, {"q": "Is POST idempotent?", "a": "Generally not; repeated POSTs can create multiple resources."}, {"q": "Is PUT idempotent?", "a": "It is designed to be idempotent when implemented according to HTTP semantics."}]}, {"g": "Templates — tell me about yourself, project explain", "items": [{"q": "Project Questions", "a": "Question What interviewer wants ------------------------------------------- ----------------------------------- Tell me about yourself. 60–90 second professional summary Explain your current project. Architecture + business purpose What exactly is your responsibility? Personal contribution Which APIs did you develop? Real backend experience Which Angular features did you implement? Frontend ownership Which DB queries/functions did you write? SQL ability Tell me about a production issue. Debugging ability Tell me about a performance issue. Optimization ability How do you deploy? DevOps understanding How does authentication work? Security understanding How does pagination work? Full-stack understanding How do you handle exceptions? Production maturity How do you log errors? Observability How do you troubleshoot DB issues? Database maturity What would you improve? Engineering judgment"}, {"q": "Tell Me About Yourself Template", "a": "“I have around 2–3 years of experience in software development, primarily working with Angular, ASP.NET Core Web APIs and PostgreSQL. In my current role, I work on frontend development, API development, database queries/functions, debugging and production support. I have also worked with Docker, Nginx, Jenkins and application monitoring/logging. My experience is mainly around developing features end-to-end and troubleshooting real production issues.” Keep it around 60–90 seconds."}, {"q": "Explain Your Project Template", "a": "Use this order: 1. What problem does the project solve? 2. Who uses it? 3. Angular role 4. .NET API role 5. PostgreSQL role 6. Authentication 7. Deployment 8. Your responsibility 9. One difficult issue Example: “The application is a mobility/transport management platform. Angular is used for the frontend, ASP.NET Core Web APIs handle business operations and PostgreSQL stores operational/reporting data. The frontend communicates with APIs through HTTP, authentication is handled through token-based security, and services are deployed using containers with reverse proxy/load-balancing infrastructure. My responsibilities include Angular development, API development, PostgreSQL functions/queries, debugging and production issue resolution.” Adapt this to exactly match your real project."}, {"q": "30 Questions To Master First", "a": "# Question What you must be able to explain ---- ----------------------------------- ---------------------------------- 1 IEnumerable vs IQueryable vs List Execution + DB 2 Deferred Execution ToList() 3 async/await I/O + threads 4 Dependency Injection Why + lifetimes 5 Scoped vs Singleton vs Transient Real examples 6 Interface vs Abstract Class When to use 7 ref/out/in Exact difference 8 Value vs Reference Type Behavior 9 Garbage Collection Gen 0/1/2 10 == vs Equals Equality 11 Middleware Request pipeline 12 Global Exception Handling Middleware 13 JWT Full authentication flow 14 Authentication vs Authorization 401 vs 403 15 ControllerBase vs Controller API vs MVC 16 HTTP status codes 200/201/400/401/403/404/409/500 17 EF Core DbContext/DbSet 18 AsNoTracking Read-only queries 19 Lazy vs Eager Loading Include() 20 N+1 Performance 21 SQL Index Benefits/trade-offs 22 EXPLAIN ANALYZE Query optimization 23 Transactions Commit/Rollback 24 ACID Real example 25 DELETE/TRUNCATE/DROP Exact difference 26 PostgreSQL MVCC/VACUUM Production DB 27 Angular Observable RxJS 28 switchMap vs mergeMap Practical use 29 Angular Interceptor JWT/error handling 30 Project Architecture Angular → API → DB FINAL INTERVIEW FORMULA For every major question, answer: 1. Definition 2. Why 3. Example 4. Real project usage 5. Trade-off / limitation Example: Question: What is Dependency Injection? Definition: Dependency Injection means dependency is provided from outside instead of the class creating it. Why: It provides loose coupling, testability and maintainability. Example: public UserService(IUserRepository repository) { _repository = repository; } Real usage: “In my project, services/repositories are injected through the ASP.NET Core DI container.” Trade-off: “Incorrect lifetime selection can cause unnecessary object creation or lifetime/thread-safety problems.” Target answer style: What → Why → Example → Real project → Follow-up."}]}];
